          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My doctor says I have type 2 diabetes.",
          "answer": "Thank you for sharing this. A diagnosis can bring a lot of questions, and it's reasonable to want to understand it better.\n\nFrom the Type 2 diabetes reference (condition-type-2-diabetes), in plain terms:\n\n- Type 2 diabetes is a problem with how the body handles blood sugar (glucose). The body's tissues respond poorly to insulin, a hormone that helps move sugar out of the blood. The pancreas, which makes insulin, cannot keep up with the extra demand. Blood sugar then stays above the normal range.\n- It usually develops gradually, over years.\n- Many people have no symptoms when it is found.\n- It is different from type 1 diabetes, where the body makes little or no insulin.\n\nThe passage I found does not cover treatment, what daily life with the condition involves, or which questions to ask your clinician. I can't answer those from the material I have.\n\nQuestions you could bring to your clinician include: What were the test results that led to this diagnosis? What does this mean for my health over time? What treatment options are there for me, and what are the benefits and side effects of each? Are there any follow-up tests or check-ups I should plan for? Where can I find reliable support or education about the condition?\n\nIf you feel very unwell, or have severe thirst, frequent urination, confusion, vomiting, or trouble breathing, seek urgent medical care right away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
