          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often should it be checked?",
          "answer": "The retrieved guidance on monitoring is from the **Type 2 diabetes: glycemic targets and monitoring intervals** document (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). It describes how often the HbA1c test, which reflects average blood sugar over about three months, is usually done:\n\n- **At least twice a year** for people who are meeting their blood sugar goal on a stable treatment plan.\n- **Every three months (quarterly)** for people who are not yet at goal, or whose treatment has recently changed.\n\nThe document also says that testing again too soon, such as at six weeks, adds little, because the result still mostly reflects the earlier period. It notes that continuous glucose monitoring and home finger-stick blood sugar checks complement HbA1c rather than replacing it.\n\nThese passages do not say which schedule applies to you. That depends on your own results, goals and treatment, so ask your clinician how often you should have your HbA1c checked and whether you need any home monitoring.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
