          "session_id": "full-mt-m-002",
          "turn_index": 2,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "And what should I be eating?",
          "answer": "The main diet passage I found comes from the **Diet for type 2 diabetes** document (lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-diet). It makes a few points:\n\n- **Weight matters.** If your body weight is above the healthy range, losing weight can help blood sugar, blood pressure and cholesterol. The passage says even a sustained loss of about 5% gives measurable benefit, and larger losses can help more. In some people, substantial weight loss puts the condition into remission. Remission means blood sugar is normal without glucose-lowering medication. It is not a cure, and monitoring continues.\n- **\"Sugar-free\" does not mean \"carbohydrate-free.\"** Starchy foods raise blood sugar about as much as sugar does.\n- **Fruit is not off-limits.** Whole fruit is part of the recommended eating patterns.\n\nThe passage also says the carbohydrate target is a decision for your own clinician, so it does not give a number.\n\nThe other passages I found were about heart failure, reflux and high blood pressure, so they don't apply directly. The adherence document (lifestyle-type-2-diabetes-adherence) adds that structured diabetes education is a core part of care and is offered at diagnosis. It is often underused. Asking your clinician or care team for a referral is a useful step.\n\nThe passages do not give a meal plan, portion sizes or specific foods to eat or avoid. A registered dietitian, who can take your health history and preferences into account, is the best person for that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
